{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He held great influence over the Crusader states and often acted as a regent for young kings. His life was filled with complex political maneuvering and military conflicts against Muslim forces. Raymond faced many challenges throughout his long tenure as a leader in the Levant. He navigated the treacherous waters of court politics while trying to maintain stability in his own domain.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born into a prominent family and inherited the county from his father Raymond II. His father had been assassinated by the Hashashin sect which left the young count vulnerable to various threats. Raymond spent his early years under the regency of his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem. She helped him secure his position despite the constant danger from enemies both inside and outside the kingdom. He eventually came of age and began to assert his own authority over the region. His rule brought a period of relative peace to Tripoli although tensions remained high with neighboring powers.\n\n*Regent of Jerusalem*\nRaymond served as regent for the young Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. This role placed him at the center of the most critical decisions facing the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He managed to balance the interests of various factions within the court while trying to prepare the kingdom for future conflicts. His relationship with the king was generally positive until rivalries with other nobles began to surface. Raymond advocated for a cautious approach to warfare and sought to avoid unnecessary battles that could weaken the Christian states. He understood the importance of diplomacy and often used negotiation to resolve disputes.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nThe later years of Raymond's life were marked by internal strife and the growing threat of Saladin. He fell out of favor with some members of the royal court due to accusations of treason. These accusations often stemmed from his refusal to support certain aggressive military campaigns. Raymond eventually retired to his own county where he continued to govern until his death. He died in 1187 just before the disastrous Battle of Hattin. His death coincided with a major shift in the balance of power in the Holy Land. The loss of such a seasoned leader left the Crusader states more vulnerable to the advancing Muslim armies. Raymond left behind a legacy of political skill and a deep understanding of the complexities of the region. His story remains an important part of the history of the Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0047274935}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.08067002}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.007453253}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.72735345}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.008732487}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.2771759}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.027933413}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.11084554}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.03214286}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.0004906666}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0038644143}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.023252096}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.009251703}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -2.3841887e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -7.629424e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.035313666}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.1828855}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -0.81440854}, {"token": " great", "logprob": -5.4476123}, {"token": " influence", "logprob": -0.0006484107}, {"token": " over", "logprob": -0.106377915}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0007027472}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.2945559}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": 